        "source_text": "\n      Definition — Linear, sublinear and quadratic convergence\n      \n        Nonnegative errors $e_k\\to0$ converge linearly (geometrically) with rate $q\\in(0,1)$ if $e_{k+1}\\le q\\,e_k$, so $e_k\\le q^ke_0$; sublinearly if slower than every geometric rate, for example $e_k=C/k^p$ with $C,p\\gt0$ and $k\\ge1$ (a polynomial upper bound alone does not rule out geometric convergence); quadratically if $e_{k+1}\\le Ce_k^2$ with $C\\gt0$ (scalar Newton's method near a regular root of a $C^2$ function, from a sufficiently close initial point).\n      \n    ",
        "text_sha256": "72f488413a92568543e7cadfd13effd90921f3dd795743e3c4ca699f1cfc9b64",
        "source_sha256": "f9b6d6adb95fb3c261768d3dc581046f7bc13e5d8c8223fd52e8ae219c68a300",
        "inventory_kind": "source_unit_needs_claim_review"
      }
    },
