        "source_text": "\n        Nonnegative errors $e_k\\to0$ converge linearly (geometrically) with rate $q\\in(0,1)$ if $e_{k+1}\\le q\\,e_k$, so $e_k\\le q^ke_0$; sublinearly if slower than every geometric rate, for example $e_k=C/k^p$ with $C,p\\gt0$ and $k\\ge1$ (a polynomial upper bound alone does not rule out geometric convergence); quadratically if $e_{k+1}\\le Ce_k^2$ with $C\\gt0$ (scalar Newton's method near a regular root of a $C^2$ function, from a sufficiently close initial point).\n      ",
        "text_sha256": "6af2bcb92475e32534740a8a4954e1e338f78ecf76b5c14a8921c78b856dc2d2",
